{\pard\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\brsp20 \adjustright \par}
\pard\plain \s3\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\cgrid 
Detailed Description\par
\pard\plain 
{
\pard\plain \s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid {\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
CPP-API: The {\b Importer} class forms an C++ interface to the functionality of the Open Asset Import Library.\par
Create an object of this class and call {\b ReadFile()} to import a file. If the import succeeds, the function returns a pointer to the imported data. The data remains property of the object, it is intended to be accessed read-only. The imported data will be destroyed along with the {\b Importer} object. If the import fails, {\b ReadFile()} returns a nullptr pointer. In this case you can retrieve a human-readable error description be calling {\b GetErrorString()}. You can call {\b ReadFile()} multiple times with a single {\b Importer} instance. Actually, constructing {\b Importer} objects involves quite many allocations and may take some time, so it's better to reuse them as often as possible.\par
If you need the {\b Importer} to do custom file handling to access the files, implement {\b IOSystem} and {\b IOStream} and supply an instance of your custom {\b IOSystem} implementation by calling {\b SetIOHandler()} before calling {\b ReadFile()}. If you do not assign a custion IO handler, a default handler using the standard C++ IO logic will be used.\par
{{\s5\sb90\sa30\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright \b\f1\fs16\cgrid 
Note\par}\pard\plain \s81\li360\widctlpar\ql\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\s17 \sa60 \sb30
One {\b Importer} instance is not thread-safe. If you use multiple threads for loading, each thread should maintain its own {\b Importer} instance. \par
}}}}
